<PageTitle>Data and execution boundary — Retry Assurance</PageTitle><span class="eyebrow">KEEP THE BOUNDARY VISIBLE</span><h1>Data &amp; execution</h1>
<section class="panel"><h2>Your code remains in your environment</h2><p>The service does not upload, build or execute arbitrary customer code. The portable .NET runner invokes your callback locally or in your CI. Use the supplied HttpClient, dummy SDK credentials and exclusively synthetic request data.</p><p>The supplied transport checks the exact HTTPS scenario origin, port, method and path before inner dispatch. It disables redirects, clears caller general/content headers, keeps only the synthetic operation key and adds fixed JSON negotiation, dummy authorization and scoped scenario credentials.</p><p>That transport is not an arbitrary-code sandbox. A program can bypass it by making its own network calls, and it does not sanitize arbitrary payload values. Adapters must not use production keys, personal data or real customer bodies. The server does not follow URLs inside a body or proxy production.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>What the service stores</h2><ul><li>Account alias, credential hashes and session metadata. Recovery credentials and capability values are not stored as plaintext.</li><li>Project membership, immutable behavior contracts and scenario content hashes.</li><li>Declared client/package/build labels and one logical operation identity per execution.</li><li>An explicit request-evidence allowlist: sequence, method/path label, attempt ID, operation-key digest, canonical payload digest, body size, server-recorded time, effect ID and response policy/status.</li><li>Actual synthetic resource records and server-derived scenario result.</li><li>The explicitly submitted bounded runner observation, labelled as runner-reported.</li></ul><p>The service does not retain raw request bodies, arbitrary request headers, production Authorization values or runner capability values. Do not put secrets in project names, behavior text, build labels or the explicit observation field.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>What the verdict means</h2><p>The server proves the synthetic effects and accepted request relationships recorded in its own journal. It does not independently attest arbitrary client source, a human identity or the runner's terminal report. A reported success must reference a resource created in that run, and it cannot erase observed duplicates.</p><p>Effect commit and journal insertion complete in an atomic database batch before delayed response headers are released. Recorded timestamps are journal times, not exact commit-completion measurements; separate computer clocks do not establish causal order. The optional runner-side callback-completion observation is labelled with its narrower scope.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>Access, revocation and deletion</h2><p>Project owners control contracts and membership. Editors can operate scoped runners; viewers read account-authorized evidence. A runner capability is tied to its creator's current owner/editor membership. Revocation, role loss or expiry blocks it and its issued child-run access.</p><p>Child-run credentials expire after thirty minutes. Finalization closes new attempt admission. Evidence remains available to authorized project members after runner revocation; it is not silently erased or presented as public data. The <a href="/account">account page</a> provides explicit deletion of the account and its owned project/run records, and removes its memberships and issued access. Active live subscriptions must end before account deletion.</p><p>Keep private recovery files and exported evidence under your own control. Exported files are independent copies; deleting server records does not rewrite a copy someone already holds.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>Billing and browser preferences</h2><p>When configured, Stripe processes checkout and subscription management. Only verified live provider records enable paid allowance. The service stores the linked provider identifiers, status and period; it does not collect card numbers itself.</p><p>Browser storage holds only a light/dark appearance preference. There are no advertising trackers, session replay scripts or third-party analytics in this workspace. Server request diagnostics exclude submitted content and credential values.</p></section>
